{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Who makes Kimah</b> — says Abraham the author: Know that there are two places on the sphere, and they are the pivots and 'Ayish, which is the Wagon, which is called the Bear; it is near the northern pivot, therefore 'Ayish is always seen in summer and winter throughout the entire northern inhabited zone. And near the southern pivot is a large red star called Canopus, and in the language of Ishmael, Suhayl. And there are small stars near it, and these are sometimes seen by anyone who dwells near the equator, but the northerners do not see any of these; therefore Scripture called them: 'the chambers of the south,' and these are called 'chambers of the south' because in the inhabited zone they are not seen. And 'Ayish is called from the derivation of עושו, for they are seven that do not separate." "<b>Now there remains to explain Kesil and Kimah</b> — and the view of our ancients: that Kimah is the tail of Aries and the head of Taurus, and they are six visible stars, though they are small. And it is known by complete proofs that the sphere of the zodiac joins with the supreme sphere at two points, and at both they are called the point of obliquity, and from there the sphere of the zodiac inclines toward the north twenty-three and a half degrees, a degree and a half their sixth, and likewise toward the south. And so they are three points, and the sun travels along the path of the zodiac sphere; therefore morning turns to darkness and day to night darkens throughout the entire inhabited zone." "<b>And the reason for calling the point Kimah</b> — is that there Kimah was in ancient days, for every hundred years the sphere of the zodiac rolls from west to east close to a degree and a half. And so in the days of Ptolemy the sage — and only a thousand years have passed since him — he found Cor Leonis at two degrees, and today it is at eighteen. And it is known that always Cor Leonis is at nine degrees relative to the form, only not relative to the joining point. And Kesil is opposite Kimah and is not one, but many, and Cor Scorpii among them. And these matters require explanation." "<b>And the reason for calling to the waters of the sea</b> — these are the clouds that rise up, and afterward He pours them upon the earth. And the one who explains it as referring to the drowning of Pharaoh — it is neither peshat nor derash, for Pharaoh did not drown on dry land. And the reason for mentioning these matters: that it was fitting for a king who is a judge to know the wisdom of HaShem and His might, and how He turns day to night and raises by His power the waters from the sea, which are below, and pours them upon the earth — and how should they not fear, for HaShem will overturn the kingdom of the judge, and an enemy will come by the command of HaShem and His decree against him to destroy his kingdom; therefore it follows after it."], :book :amos, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "Resolved 'the author' as Ibn Ezra's standard self-reference; the astronomical terms (סדנים/pivots, עש/'Ayish) follow his usage in other commentaries.", 3 "The final phrase 'ולהשחית ממלכתו על כן אחריו' is read as Ibn Ezra's pointer to the following verse; the syntax is slightly compressed and possibly truncated in the manuscript tradition."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Amos -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/amos"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:18:16.624271306Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}